;;; `open` written as a premise pattern's argument loads, as a name of the
;;; program — M1f **W71**, register row `W71/premise-value`, filed by S1f.5.23
;;; (T1f.5.23.2, 2026-11-10).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `r`'s premise `(p open)` asks for the fact `p` holds of `open`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `open` is the verdict a rule's `:assert` states, `(open)` or `(open ?R)`,
;;; and is written nowhere else (the user's ruling on Q-M1f.66, 2026-11-10):
;;; here it is written as a premise pattern's argument, so the program is
;;; refused at the parse, at the `open`, and every command exits 1 (`ein test`:
;;; 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `f62e34e1`, release: the program loads, `open` is a name of the program,
;;; so `(p open)` is a candidate and `r` fires under it: R is 22 states, exit 0
;;; on every command.
;;; Refused at the parse since S1f.5.23 (T1f.5.23.3).

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(rule r ()
  :match  (and (a ?x) (p open))
  :assert (q ?x)
  :why "{?x} is an a, and open is a p, so {?x} is a q")
